{#
  VRTouchEvents - JUST CAME TO.                     (2026-09-12, the user's design)

  A choke that reaches 15 s knocks her out, and VRTE's KO slot wakes her later - a heal
  (potion, spell) or the 2-4 game-hour timer. Nothing else ever tells SkyrimNet she is
  conscious again: the last thing in her history is the passout. Measured 2026-09-12 -
  every reply after a wake was still "Carmella is unconscious.", even while she was being
  choked again. This block is the missing "she woke up".

  The user's wording: "(NPC's name) just woke up from being unconscious, they are slightly
  disoriented at first and are trying to regain their senses" - so the NPC acts like
  someone who was just out cold.

  ★★★★ THE GATE IS THE LIVE STATE FILE (2026-09-12, fourth design - see 0796): read_json of
  VRTouchEvents/prompt_state, state 4, written by VRTouchEvents.dll the moment she wakes; it comes off after her 2 SPOKEN replies (counted by
  VRTouchEvents.dll from SkyrimNet's "dialogue" event) or after 60 s, whichever first.
  ⛔ The 60 s limit is the fix for VR test 2: the C++-decorator version first showed this block
  2 m 45 s after Carmella woke, while another NPC was being choked, and she played it as waking
  right then. Being choked again sets rank 1, so this block and 0796 never render together.

  ★ ENFORCE, DO NOT NEGATE (user, 2026-09-08): no "do not / never / cannot / can not /
    no longer" in the rendered prose. Comments are exempt.
  ★ FACTUAL: the state of the body and the senses. How she takes it follows from who she is.
  ★ Names or he/she (from decnpc), never they/their.
  ⚠ A faction rank is a real number (a non-member reads below 0) - no isString guard needed.
  ⚠ Set the name into a variable before upper-casing it - see 0796's note.

  Renders in EVERY mode, thoughts included: a mind coming back online is exactly what her
  inner monologue should carry. (0796 skips thoughts because a choke stops speech, not thought.)

  Numbering: 0798 follows 0796 / 0797 and sits before SkyrimNet's 0800_direct_narration.
  ★★★★★ THE CACHE-BUST (2026-09-13, VR test 5 + the user). SkyrimNet caches EVERY template call by name + argument
  value (55 s default, 30 s from a voice key press), so a plain read_json of this file was served stale on 2 of 7
  renders - the modified-time check inside read_json never runs on a cache hit. The second argument is three
  fresh random values (random is never cached): a number, so read_json ignores it and returns the whole file
  (SkyrimNet.dll 0x697500 - a path is used only when that argument is a string), but it is part of the cache key,
  so nearly every render is a new key and a real read. VERIFIED LIVE 2026-09-13 20:45 (VR test 8, report 45): the DLL wrote the state, the
  render 189 ms later logged 'File modified since cached, reloading' and this block was in that prompt; 22 of 22
  renders in the session ran the read (0 served stale by the callback cache).
  To revert: drop the second argument.
  ★ GAG (2026-09-13, fix list 41 V6, the user: "V6 yes"). A GAGGED NPC gets no wake block: its "first words come
  haltingly" sat against DD SN's 0795 gag block. Same native gate as 0797 (zad_DeviousGag / zbfWornGag / DOMWornGag).
  Thoughts still render (see above).
#}
{% if exists("npc") and existsIn(npc, "UUID") %}
{% set _vs = read_json("VRTouchEvents/prompt_state", random * 10000 + random * 100 + random) %}
{% set _vk_state = 0 %}
{% if isObject(_vs) and existsIn(_vs, "npcs") %}{% for _ve in _vs.npcs %}{% if _ve.uuid == npc.UUID %}{% set _vk_state = _ve.state %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}{% endif %}
{% if _vk_state == 4 and not worn_has_keyword(npc.UUID, "zad_DeviousGag") and not worn_has_keyword(npc.UUID, "zbfWornGag") and not worn_has_keyword(npc.UUID, "DOMWornGag") %}
{% set _p = "he" %}{% set _pp = "his" %}{% set _po = "him" %}
{% if decnpc(npc.UUID).isFemale %}{% set _p = "she" %}{% set _pp = "her" %}{% set _po = "her" %}{% endif %}
{% set _vk_name = decnpc(npc.UUID).name %}
**JUST CAME TO - {{ _vk_name | upper }} WAS UNCONSCIOUS MOMENTS AGO.** {{ _vk_name }} has just woken up after being choked unconscious and is only now coming back to {{ _pp }} senses.

The first moments are foggy and slow. {{ _pp | capitalize }} head swims, {{ _pp }} vision takes a moment to sharpen, and {{ _pp }} throat is raw and sore. {{ _p | capitalize }} is slightly disoriented at first, piecing together where {{ _p }} is and what just happened, and {{ _pp }} first words and movements come haltingly while {{ _pp }} senses return.

How {{ _p }} takes what was done to {{ _po }}, and what {{ _p }} says, follows from who {{ _p }} is.
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
